#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Generates today's digests via an LLM, clustering the day's material
# into however many topics it actually falls into, and writes them (plus
# per-topic extracted references) to ~/.cache/newsdigest-server/digest/
# for the app to pull from /feed/digests.
#
# This is an EXAMPLE - copy it outside the repo (it's not meant to be run
# as-is) and fill in the marked section with your own personalization.
# A good digest should know who you are and what you actually care about
# to pick out what's worth mentioning; that's inherently personal, so it
# doesn't belong in a public repo even as a real working default.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")"  # so `python3 -c 'import feed'` below resolves feed.py

SOURCE_FILES=$(python3 -c "
import feed
paths = sorted({s['items_file'] for s in feed._enabled_sources()})
print('\n'.join(paths))
")

TMP=$(mktemp)
trap 'rm -f "$TMP"' EXIT

claude --dangerously-skip-permissions -p "$(cat <<PROMPT
Read today's new activity from these JSONL files (one JSON object per
line; fields include title, summary, feed_title, tags, link, published):

$SOURCE_FILES

Filter to items published or fetched in roughly the last 24 hours.

# --- fill in your own personalization here ---
# e.g. "skim ~/notes/about-me.md for context on who I am and what I
# actually care about" - whatever tells the model which of today's items
# are actually worth your time, and how they connect to what you're
# working on.

Group today's items into whatever topics they actually fall into - do NOT
use a fixed set of categories. Look at what's actually there and let the
grouping fall out of it: some days might genuinely split into several
distinct topics, other days might just be one broad "today" if there's
no real separation. The number and nature of topics should depend
entirely on today's material, never on a template. Don't force an item
into a topic it doesn't belong in just to fill one out, and don't create
a topic for one throwaway item - fold stragglers into the closest
reasonable topic or a general one.

First, write ONE overview covering the whole day across every topic
below - this is the main, thorough read, not a teaser: aim for roughly
1,800-3,000 words, enough to read aloud in about 10 to 20 minutes at a
natural pace. Go into real detail within it on each topic - actual
narrative substance and the connections between items, the way the
detailed topics below do - not a single highlight sentence each. Give it
a genuinely descriptive title of its own (like a newsletter issue
headline capturing what actually stood out today), not just the word
"Overview". Then write each of the detailed topics.

Write EACH detailed topic as its own short piece of narrative writing -
like an entry in a personal newsletter written directly to you, not a
bulleted report. Within a topic, let one item flow into the next with
real transitions, the way someone telling you about their day would,
rather than restarting structure for each item. A few paragraphs per
topic is plenty; a topic with only one or two items can be a single
short paragraph. These are meant to be quicker, narrower reads than the
overview - someone who only cares about one area reaches for these
instead of the whole thing.

For every topic including the overview, also pick one short, broad
category label - a couple of words at most (e.g. "Architecture",
"Programming", "Career", "Security", "Science") - that classifies what
kind of topic it is at a glance. The overview's category can be
something like "Overview" or "Daily Summary" itself. This is separate
from the topic's own descriptive title - the category is a coarse
at-a-glance tag, the title is specific to today's actual content.

Every story or item you mention by name must carry a markdown link
[label](url) to its original link field, when it has one - never mention
a linkable item without linking it, and never invent a URL that isn't in
the source files. Also name where each item is FROM in the prose itself,
using its feed_title field - not only via the link - since someone
reading this aloud through text-to-speech never sees a URL or its domain.

Output format - this is machine-parsed, follow it exactly: separate each
topic with a line reading exactly "===TOPIC: <descriptive topic
title>===" immediately followed by a line reading exactly "===CATEGORY:
<short category label>===" (no markdown formatting on either line),
then that topic's narrative. The overview goes first. Nothing else
outside that structure - no preamble, no overall title, no
meta-commentary. Your entire response is saved verbatim and split on
those markers.

Example shape (not real content):
===TOPIC: A Quiet Day Except For Two Big Commits===
===CATEGORY: Overview===
<the long, detailed overview narrative touching every topic below>
===TOPIC: Three Recruiters Went Quiet This Week===
===CATEGORY: Career===
<narrative for this topic>
===TOPIC: Claude Code Ships Worktree Support===
===CATEGORY: Programming===
<narrative for this topic>
PROMPT
)" > "$TMP"

if [ ! -s "$TMP" ]; then
    echo "generate-digest: claude produced no output, not writing" >&2
    exit 1
fi

python3 build_digest_json.py < "$TMP"
